                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Kitchen remodel (mid-range)</td><td>$35,000 – $75,000</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Kitchen remodel (luxury/custom cabinets)</td><td>$75,000 – $150,000+</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Bathroom remodel</td><td>$15,000 – $45,000</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Room addition (single story)</td><td>$80,000 – $140,000</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Second-story addition</td><td>$150,000 – $250,000+</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Full-home renovation</td><td>$150,000 – $400,000+</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Mudroom / built-in cabinetry</td><td>$8,000 – $25,000</td></tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>

              <p>These ranges reflect fully permitted, professional-grade work. Bids well below these ranges often exclude permits, use non-union subcontractors without proper insurance, or plan to substitute materials mid-project. Always compare apples to apples: what materials, what permits, and what subcontractors are included in each quote?</p>
              <p>General contractor fees are typically built into the project price as a markup on materials and subcontractor labor (10–20%) or as a flat management fee. Ask your contractor to explain their fee structure explicitly.</p>
            </div>

